From e580fed15ecb5f419d45855026f1b6884330d0b4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 01:10:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Keep a numeric block-scoped var in a raw double, completing
 item 3-3

A `var` declared inside a block is hoisted to the function, so between entry
and the block its binding is observably `undefined` -- which a raw double
hoisted to 0 answers as 0. That is a silent wrong answer, and it is why this
half of item 3-3 was left needing "definite-assignment analysis".

What it needs is the dominance argument the function body already gets, applied
one level down, and it admits two shapes:

  * TRANSPARENT -- an unlabelled `{ ... }` that is a direct statement of the
    function body, or of another transparent block. It is entered whenever
    control reaches it and the only exits are `return`/`throw`, which leave the
    function, so it does not weaken the body's dominance and its vars carry no
    extra condition.
  * CONFINED -- a var that is a direct statement of any other block, where every
    reference is inside that block and after it. Entering the block is then what
    proves the initializer ran. This is the loop-body temporary the item is
    really about.

Only a DIRECT statement of the block qualifies. Keying on the innermost
enclosing block instead would admit `if (c) var t = 1; return t;`, whose
enclosing block does not dominate the declaration. A labelled block is excluded
because `break` can leave it before the declaration runs, and a `catch` because
it is a sibling of its `try`'s block rather than inside it.

Measured with --local-alloc, both arms from one tree: `block-var` goes
31.98 -> 0.00 B/iteration and 1 -> 3 numeric locals, matching `top-level-var`,
with all twelve other rows byte-identical -- one row moved, which is the whole
diff. All four of item 3-3's categories are now at the eligible floor except
`parameter`, which cannot reach the numeric tier at all.

Two defects were caught by testing and neither shipped. A name could be marked
readable at a NON-dominating declaration, masking a read between it and the
dominating one that can still see `undefined`; a name now becomes readable at
its dominating declaration only. The fix for that then over-corrected into
rejecting `var s = 0; { var s = 5; }` as declared-twice, which
ANumericReDeclarationKeepsTheLocalSpecialized caught -- a test whose comment
reads "this is the guard against over-fixing". Two declarations that both
dominate are not a hazard, and that rejection is gone.

Adds BlockScopedVarNumericLocalTests (43 cases), whose refusals are each
written as a value the program can observe: String(t) answering "undefined"
where a raw double answers "0".
